  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1151 AM MST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nModels are in good agreement with satellite imagery showing a deep \ncold low well off the coast of the Pacific Northwest/Northern \nCalifornia starting to make the turn to the east to move inland over \nOregon/Northern California this evening into Thursday pushing a well \ndeveloped  active frontal boundary inland ahead of it while the \nsystem that went through last night has moved out onto the Northern \nPlains. The weak transient ridge between these two systems is moving \nout of the Great Basin into the Rocky Mountains with the lingering \nshowers and clouds across the northern mountains this morning \nquickly evaporating under the subsidence from the ridge. As the low \nto the west moves in through the Great Basin Thursday, it \ntransitions into an open wave trough tracking across eastern Utah \nand Western Colorado Friday into Saturday. Look for the cold front \nto push through the region overnight Thursday into Friday morning \nwith snow levels dropping to 6000 feet across the northern areas to \n7000 feet across the southern areas. As the moisture associated with \nthis system comes through the Pacific Northwest with the Front, \nthere isn't a lot of it available, and thus there won't be major \nimpacts as this system moves through. Expect light to moderate snow \nshowers mostly through the northern and central mountains overnight \nThursday into Friday with four to eight inches possible on the \nwestern mountain faces and only one to three inches forecast on \nRabbit Ears and Vail Passes. There is still a lot of variance in the \nmodels, especially on timing of this system moving through, but the \nworst impacts look to be Friday morning. Even though snow amounts \nare on the light side, ground/road surface temperatures have cooled. \nExpect possible icy and/or snowpacked conditions on the higher \nmountain passes mostly across the northern half of the region. \nAllow extra time if you will be traveling through these areas \nThursday night and Friday. \n\nModels agree on this system shifting the storm track/jet stream \nfarther south over eastern Utah and Western Colorado bringing more \nunsettled weather to the region through the coming Thanksgiving week \nand possibly beyond. Expect a disturbance passing through the region \nevery few days with minor impacts to travel mostly through the \nmountain areas. Stay tuned for updates as the timing of these \ndisturbances is indeterminant beyond the first few days of the \nforecast. What you can expect is that temperatures will cool about \nten degrees Friday and beyond with the daily highs and low \nabout five degrees below the seasonal normal.",
